",0,2006],["東藻琴村",0,2006]],"01584":[["洞爺村",0,2006],["虻田町",0,2006]],"01585":[["早来町",0,2006],["追分町",0,2006]],"01586":[["穂別町",0,2006],["鵡川町",0,2006]],"01601":[["日高町",0,0],["門別町",0,2006]],"01610":[["三石町",0,2006],["静内町",0,2006]],"01643":[["幕別町",0,0],["忠類村",0,2006]]},"yurai":{"1":{"札幌市":"Written \"satsuhoro\" in 17th-century records and \"札縨\" by the late Edo period; in Meiji the second character became 幌, giving 札幌. The name is generally traced to Ainu.","旭川市":"The old name, Chūbetsu, came from the Ainu \"chuppet\". Before the village opened, the Tondenhei officer Okabe Hōki told a newspaper that \"Asahi\" (rising sun) kept the \"sun\" meaning of the Ainu name while also evoking the Empire.","函館市":"In 1454 the Tsugaru lord Kōno Masamichi built a fort at Usukeshi on the slope of Mount Hakodate; it was box-shaped (hako), hence 箱館. In Meiji it became 函館 — said to be the colonial commissioner Higashikuze Michitomi's preference, though the date is disputed.","苫小牧市":"The Ainu \"makomai\" (river leading into the hills), with \"to\" (marsh) added, is the usual etymology. It first appears as \"Tofumakomafu\" in Matsuura Takeshirō's diary; written 苫細 in 1873, it became 苫小牧 the same year.","釧路市":"Known in Edo times as \"Kusuri\" (written 久摺 or 久寿里) and renamed 釧路 in Meiji. It is Ainu, but the meaning — a crossing path, medicine water, a through-river — is disputed.","帯広市":"From the start of the Obihiro River's Ainu name, \"o-pere-perke-p\" (the river whose mouth splits many ways), plus \"hiro\" for the breadth of the Tokachi plain.","北見市":"Renamed from Nokkeushi when it became a city in 1942, as the commercial centre of Kitami Province. Nokkeushi rendered the Ainu \"nup-kes\", \"end of the plain\".","小樽市":"From the Ainu \"ota-or-nay\", river in the sand — originally the lower Hoshioki River on today's Sapporo border. The Matsumae domain's Otarunai trading post moved to sheltered Kuttar-ush, today's city centre, and took the name with it.","千歳市":"The Ainu called the river \"shikot-pet\" and the area Shikotsu. In 1805 officials thought it sounded like \"dead bones\" (shikotsu); Hakodate magistrate Habuto Masayasu, noting the many red-crowned cranes, renamed it Chitose — \"a thousand years\", from the saying that cranes live a thousand years.","室蘭市":"Today's centre was called Tokkarimoi; when the port moved here from Moruran (now Sakimori) in 1871, the name came too. Written 室蘭, it was read \"Mororan\" until mid-Meiji, then drifted to \"Muroran\" after the kanji. Moruran may be Ainu for \"little slope\".","岩見沢市":"Road builders on the Sapporo–Horonai route bathed (yuami) at a rest house on the Ikushunbetsu River, so it was called \"Yuamizawa\", which became Iwamizawa — a rare Hokkaido city name from Japanese rather than Ainu.","石狩市":"Ainu, most likely describing the winding Ishikari River. Nagata Hōsei's glossary records several readings, from \"meandering river\" to \"beautifully made\".","北広島市":"Settled in 1884 by 25 families from Hiroshima. They wanted to name it after their leader Wada Ikujirō, who refused, so it became \"Hiroshima clearing\" and in 1894 Hiroshima Village. The station was called Kita-Hiroshima to tell it apart, and the city followed."}},"kyu":{},"famous":{"01100":[["Q539725","Chūhei Nambu",1904,1997,"南部忠平"],["Q2590475","Masayuki Mori",1911,1973,"森雅之 (俳優)"],["Q432850","Shigeru Oda",1924,2025,"小田滋"],["Q7385560","Ryuzo Yanagimachi",1928,2023,"柳町隆造"],["Q6983732","Shigeru Tamura",1909,1987,"田村茂"],["Q6455065","Kō Nishimura",1923,1997,"西村晃"]],"01202":[["Q290963","Hideko Takamine",1924,2010,"高峰秀子"],["Q385688","Kazuo Ohno",1906,2010,"大野一雄"],["Q2571077","Gorō Naya",1929,2013,"納谷悟朗"],["Q2004317","Kōgo Noda",1893,1968,"野田高梧"],["Q460613","Juran Hisao",1902,1957,"久生十蘭"],["Q1647940","Ippei Okamoto",1886,1948,"岡本一平"]],"01203":[["Q232588","Miyoshi Umeki",1929,2007,"ミヨシ・ウメキ"],["Q445807","Masaki Kobayashi",1916,1996,"小林正樹"],["Q3551306","Nobuo Nakamura",1908,1991,"中村伸郎"],["Q2311412","Hideo Oguma",1901,1940,"小熊秀雄"],["Q3297135","Masaji Iguro",1913